aight line segments. Each segment has its own colour and its own length, and none of them touch. Some run horizontally and some vertically.\n\n**What the output is made of.** Every output is a square of concentric frames, a bit like a target. Every colour in it comes from one of the segments.\n\n**Linking the two (read from the fact sheet):**\n- **Pair 1:** the segment lengths are 1→6, 3→4 and 6→2. The output is 6×6, with 1 as the outer frame, 3 as the next frame and 6 as the centre.\n- **Pair 2:** the lengths are 2→5, 3→4 and 8→1. The output is 5×5, with frames 2, then 3, then 8 at the centre.\n- **Pair 3:** the lengths are 8→8, 3→6, 4→4 and 7→2. The output is 8×8, with frames 8, 3, 4, 7 from the outside in.\n\nSo the rule is:\n- The output is a square whose side equals the length of the longest segment.\n- Sort the segments from longest to shortest.\n- Frame k counts inward from the border, starting at 0. It gets the colour of the k-th longest segment.\n\nThe square's side is what makes the shape work. In pair 2 the side is odd (5), so the centre is a single cell. That is why colour 3 shows up as a 3×3 ring even though its segment has 4 cells. Only the ordering of the lengths matters, plus the longest length, which sets the size.\n\nTo draw it, give each cell (y, x) the depth `min(y, x, n-1-y, n-1-x)`, which is its distance to the nearest edge. Paint the cell with the colour at that position in the sorted list. If the depth runs past the end of the list, clip it to the last colour. This rule reproduces all three outputs exactly.\n\n**Test input.** The segments are 8 (length 7), 6 (length 5), 7 (length 3) and 3 (length 1). That gives a 7×7 output with 8 on the border, then a 6 ring, then a 7 ring, and a single 3 in the centre.\n\n**Construction stages.**\n1. Allocate an n×n canvas filled with the colour of the longest segment, which is the outer frame.\n2. Paint each inner frame in turn, emitting a canvas after each one.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    objs = sorted(objs, key=lambda o: -o[\"size\"])\n    cols = [o[\"colour\"] for o in objs]\n    n = objs[0][\"size\"]\n    out = np.full((n, n), cols[0], dtype=int)\n    emit(out, f\"Allocate {n}x{n} square (longest segment length) filled with its colour {cols[0]}.\")\n    k = 1\n    while k < len(cols) and k <= (n - 1) // 2:\n        out[k:n - k, k:n - k] = cols[k]\n        emit(out, f\"Paint frame {k} (and everything inside) with colour {cols[k]}, the next longest segment.\")\n        k += 1\n    emit(out, \"Final nested frames.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The output is a square whose side equals the length of the longest segment."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Sort the segments from longest to shortest."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It gets the colour of the k-th longest segment."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Frame k counts inward from the border, starting at 0."}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Sort the segments from longest to shortest."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Paint the cell with the colour at that position in the sorted list."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Allocate an n×n canvas filled with the colour of the longest segment, which is the outer frame."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the output is made of.** Every output is a square of concentric frames, a bit like a target."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"To draw it, give each cell (y, x) the depth `min(y, x, n-1-y, n-1-x)`, which is its distance to the nearest edge."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"f13f8ad224f0cd34b1939a83278fa55d135c172cd44401b08f7758d3ce285499","task_sha256":"34c09d16ecc009b550d24a737ad65158d94575ffe61fa030746a50474c7dd251","validation":"Frozen corpus reco